lesson.body:
Polymorphism literally means "many forms." In object-oriented programming, it describes what you already saw at the end of the method overriding section: the same method call, `.send(...)` for instance, produces different behavior depending on which class the object actually is. Polymorphism is not a new syntax feature, it is the natural result of combining two things you already know: inheritance, which gives a family of classes a shared set of method names, and method overriding, which lets each class in that family fill in its own version of what those methods actually do.

The payoff shows up when you write code that uses those objects. You can loop over a list containing many different subclasses and call the same method on every single one, without writing a single `if` statement to check which subclass you have.

```python
class Shape:
    def area(self):
        raise NotImplementedError

class Circle(Shape):
    def __init__(self, radius):
        self.radius = radius

    def area(self):
        return round(3.14159 * self.radius ** 2, 2)

class Rectangle(Shape):
    def __init__(self, width, height):
        self.width = width
        self.height = height

    def area(self):
        return self.width * self.height

shapes = [Circle(3), Rectangle(4, 5)]
for s in shapes:
    print(f"{type(s).__name__}: {s.area()}")
```

This prints `Circle: 28.27` and then `Rectangle: 20`. The loop body has exactly one line, `s.area()`, and yet it correctly computes a circle's area with pi times radius squared and a rectangle's area with width times height. The loop itself has no idea which specific shape it is looking at on any given pass, and it does not need to. It only relies on a shared contract: every Shape promises an `area` method. Each object honors that promise in its own way, which is polymorphism at work.

This matters enormously for real programs because it makes code open to extension without needing modification. If you later add a Triangle class that also inherits from Shape and overrides `area`, the loop above keeps working exactly as written, no changes required, as long as Triangle honors the same `area()` contract. Notice, though, that this example still relies on Circle and Rectangle sharing a common parent class, Shape. Python actually takes this idea one step further and does not require a shared parent class at all for polymorphism to work, which is the subject of the next section: duck typing.
